    "conclusion": "Equal-weight (no direction filter) vs current (with D111 direction filter) differs in 3/11 experiments. The difference is driven by NaiveXCorr having significance with peak_lag > 0 (reverse direction), which passes significance-only check but is filtered by D111. Critical: null experiment (a=0.00) inflates from 0 to 2 K>=3 pairs without direction filter. Direction filtering is essential for FPR control. At strong signal (a>=0.50 T=10), both methods agree perfectly at K3 level. Largest divergence at t20/a045 (+4 pairs) where NaiveXCorr is broadly significant but often in wrong direction."
